# runwAI scan exclusions.
#
# controls/tests/ holds fixtures that contain deliberate vulnerabilities,
# annotated with `ruleid:` and `ok:` so every rule is asserted in both directions.
# They are exercised by `semgrep --test`, never by a normal scan — scanning them
# would fail the gate on purpose-built bad code.
controls/tests/

# The ISM snapshot and the published text it was taken from are large data files, not
# source. ism-source.txt is kept as the provenance artefact for the snapshot.
controls/ism-snapshot.json
controls/ism-source.txt
controls/ism-index.json

# Nothing else is excluded. The adopter-facing configs at the root — biome.json,
# playwright.config.ts, promptfooconfig.yaml — are deliberately in scope. Our rulesets are
# only worth shipping to the extent an adopter's agent runs them over an adopter's files,
# and these are the first such files in the tree. A rule that would be noisy on generic
# config is a rule that needs fixing, not a path that needs hiding.

.git/

# The dependency audit's working directory: two scanner binaries of a couple of hundred
# megabytes each, and a vulnerability database of about a gigabyte. Gitignored, so semgrep
# already skips it by default — named here as well because a run with --no-git-ignore
# would otherwise try to read all of it.
.audit-cache/

# The toolchain `make setup` installs. Gitignored, so semgrep already skips it — named
# here for the same reason .audit-cache/ is, because a run with --no-git-ignore would
# otherwise scan several thousand files of third-party code and report on scanners rather
# than on this repository.
.venv/

# .runwai/ holds runwAI's own development record — backlog, decisions, provenance. That
# is documentation about building the template, not code shipped by it.
#
# Excluded by file type rather than as a directory, and the difference matters. `.runwai/`
# used to be here as a whole, which was harmless while the tree held only Markdown and
# YAML. Decision 3 then moved four Python self-checks into .runwai/tools/ and they left
# the scanner's scope without anyone deciding they should — measured, not guessed: semgrep
# reported two target files where it should have seen six. A re-include (`!.runwai/tools/`)
# does not fix it, because semgrep follows gitignore semantics and a file cannot be
# re-included once its parent directory is excluded.
#
# Decision 2 says our own rules apply to what we ship. That has to include the code we ship
# them with.
.runwai/*.md
.runwai/*.yaml
